}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}
\itemize{
\item \strong{relation} --- run-by-condition effect estimates. Build one from beta
matrices with \code{\link[=relation]{relation()}}, or fit one from scan responses with
\code{\link[=plan_relation]{plan_relation()}} and \code{\link[=estimate_relation]{estimate_relation()}}.
\item \strong{frame} --- where results are reported. Choose \code{\link[=searchlights]{searchlights()}},
\code{\link[=regions]{regions()}}, \code{\link[=voxelwise]{voxelwise()}}, or \code{\link[=whole_brain]{whole_brain()}}, and compile it against a
neural domain with \code{\link[=compile_frame]{compile_frame()}}. A \emph{measurement} is one such spatial
unit; every result has one row per measurement.
\item \strong{pairing} --- which partitions must generalize, declared with
\code{\link[=cross_partitions]{cross_partitions()}}. This is bound into the plan's identity, so
generalizing over runs and over sessions are different estimands.
\item \strong{geometry plan} --- what \code{\link[=plan_geometry]{plan_geometry()}} returns. It records the
estimand (the quantity you mean to estimate) before any neural values are
read, and keeps that identity separate from execution details such as
block size or storage.
\item \strong{views} --- \code{\link[=contrast_energy]{contrast_energy()}}, \code{\link[=rdm]{rdm()}}, \code{\link[=rsa]{rsa()}}, and \code{\link[=crossnobis]{crossnobis()}},
